<p>NASA unveiled the Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope, a new observatory built to scan huge swaths of the sky for exoplanets and to probe dark matter and dark energy. Launch preparations are now moving toward Florida ahead of a SpaceX mission planned for September at the earliest. The telescope took more than a decade to build and cost more than $4 billion. It is named after Nancy Grace Roman, NASA’s first chief astronomer and a key figure in the development of Hubble. NASA says Roman will serve as a new “atlas of the universe,” with a field of view at least 100 times larger than Hubble’s, allowing it to survey vast regions of space. Scientists expect it to find tens of thousands of planets beyond the solar system, making it one of NASA’s most ambitious efforts yet to answer some of astronomy’s biggest open questions.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/mPIkLItW-QDxOLEBFQ0KjHocb3DqTT0WjwwjEDtz3qdzBA7CQcM7JgPlwkensqbGFKDHqU3xBmQR-ykFEtFuFAYq7Mf5djLOqQl6EPGwtR_c' alt='The Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope is unveiled to the public at NASA’s Goddard Space Flight Center in Greenbelt, Maryland on April 21, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Launch window: NASA plans to ship the observatory to Florida for launch aboard a SpaceX rocket, with sources describing the earliest target as September and other reports saying launch is expected in late 2026 or early 2027.</li><li>Physical scale: NASA unveiled the telescope as a 12-metre, or 39-foot, silver-colored structure with large solar panels, highlighting the scale of the mission hardware now entering its final pre-launch phase.</li><li>Legacy link: Roman’s name honors Nancy Grace Roman, often called the “Mother of Hubble,” tying the new mission to the earlier telescope that transformed modern astronomy 36 years ago.</li></ul><blockquote>Roman will give the Earth a new atlas of the universe - Jared Isaacman</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Jared Isaacman: The NASA administrator described the telescope as 